## 8. Network mocking

**Standard:** Use `page.route` + `route.fulfill()` (or `route.abort()` for network failure) for deterministic 503/500, empty list, malformed JSON, and related edge cases — recon real copy first, one tag per test, POM-only interaction, never mock the happy-path call you are proving.

✅ **Good** (*advised — add under `tests/` per `.cursor/skills/programs-network-mocks/SKILL.md`; no routed spec in repo yet*):

```typescript
test('empty catalog shows empty state', { tag: '@e2e' }, async ({ page }) => {
  await page.route('**/api/programs', async (route) => {
    if (route.request().method() === 'GET') {
      await route.fulfill({ status: 200, contentType: 'application/json', body: '[]' });
      return;
    }
    await route.continue();
  });

  const programs = new ProgramsPage(page);
  await programs.goto();
  await expect(programs.emptyState).toBeVisible();
});
```

❌ **Bad:**

```typescript
test('create program', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.route('**/api/programs', (route) => route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: '{"id":"fake"}' }));
  // mocks POST while asserting real create success
});
```

**Why:** Wrong mocks hide integration bugs; routing only the failure path keeps the scenario honest and repeatable in CI.

